      },
      "lineNbr": 374,
      "author": {
        "id": 1004831
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-02-12T15:07:20Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e So it seems that we need DRLR :/ which has only very little support even on Android.\n\nFor \"unresolve\" MSAA logic we need either DRLR or MSRTSS. MSRTSS has better support on Android so it should allow us to use dynamic rendering for most devices that support it.\n\nFor framebufer fetch we probably do need DRLR.",
      "parentUuid": "b52a1b4e_a9d9ae4a",
      "revId": "abbcbf94a1d3e9ee8c789632f83b931a13d37c1e",
      "serverId": "dd02978d-1a8e-36d7-bcc0-a5723e5c0abd"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "2f807d01_eadd2722",
        "filename": "src/dawn/native/vulkan/ResolveTextureLoadingUtilsVk.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 374,
      "author": {
        "id": 1002296
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-02-12T15:22:02Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "To Kyle\u0027s point, on TBRs you should be able to get MSRTSS in lots of devices (practically, a superset of devices that have DRLR). And on IMRs you don\u0027t actually need DRLR, you can do the unresolve in a separate pass (by texture sampling even), for example on Exynos GPUs or various Android emulators.\n\n---\n\nBy the way, https://registry.khronos.org/vulkan/specs/latest/html/vkspec.html has become excrutiatingly slow to load. There\u0027s a replacement at https://docs.vulkan.org/spec/latest/index.html",
      "parentUuid": "a32c59cd_5b9a92c2",
      "revId": "abbcbf94a1d3e9ee8c789632f83b931a13d37c1e",
      "serverId": "dd02978d-1a8e-36d7-bcc0-a5723e5c0abd"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "64c6e914_49cb9ffe",
        "filename": "src/dawn/native/vulkan/ResolveTextureLoadingUtilsVk.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 374,
      "author": {
        "id": 1001591
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-02-12T22:03:57Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The complication with doing a second pass is if provided multisample texture is transient (which I believe all of Graphite\u0027s uses are) then the first pass resolve won\u0027t won\u0027t persist to the second pass. We could silently swap in a non-transient texture on the backend but then we\u0027re defeating the intent behind using the transient texture in the first place.\n\nWe have the opposite problem if using MSRTSS: If the multisample texture provided is _not_ transient then we can\u0027t use MSRTSS to implement, because you wouldn\u0027t get the appropriate content in the multisample target as a side effect of the pass. We could restrict LoadResolveTexture to only use transient textures, but then it\u0027s just MSRTSS with more steps AND still has the problem described above when using a two-pass implementation. 😕",
      "parentUuid": "2f807d01_eadd2722",
      "revId": "abbcbf94a1d3e9ee8c789632f83b931a13d37c1e",
      "serverId": "dd02978d-1a8e-36d7-bcc0-a5723e5c0abd"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "021166d6_ee667363",
        "filename": "src/dawn/native/vulkan/ResolveTextureLoadingUtilsVk.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 374,
      "author": {
        "id": 1002296
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-02-13T03:33:05Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e but then we\u0027re defeating the intent behind using the transient texture in the first place\n\nExcept transient textures don\u0027t exist on IMRs to begin with. And FWIW MSRTSS _behavior_ on IMRs is not only useless, it\u0027s hurtful.\n\nIf you have the option, ideally you\u0027d want to _not_ expose MSRTSS behavior where transient memory doesn\u0027t exist and let the application use real MSAA images with STORE_OP_STORE and future LOAD_OP_LOAD. That\u0027ll be faster than adding an unresolve pass and pretending MSAA data is discarded (because it mostly isn\u0027t). Then you never need to bother with a two-pass implementation. If the app needs to turn single sampled into MSAA, it\u0027s able to do it itself.\n\nIf you have to support MSRTSS behavior (because simplicity for application or whatever), then two-pass implementation on IMR is no different from a single-pass implementation with a barrier in the middle.",
      "parentUuid": "64c6e914_49cb9ffe",
      "revId": "abbcbf94a1d3e9ee8c789632f83b931a13d37c1e",
      "serverId": "dd02978d-1a8e-36d7-bcc0-a5723e5c0abd"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "491d3298_13b86ee7",
        "filename": "src/dawn/native/vulkan/ResolveTextureLoadingUtilsVk.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 374,
      "author": {
        "id": 1001591
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-02-13T18:39:23Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Sounds like an argument for not supporting LoadResolveTexture at all, and just exposing MSRTSS when the hardware advertises it? (Presumably IMRs won\u0027t advertise an extension that\u0027s harmful on their hardware.)\n\nAs of a couple of days ago Graphite will now use MSRTSS when available (Dawn only reports it when the Vulkan extension is supported), and fall back to using LoadResolveTexture with transient multisample targets when it\u0027s not. (Done currently with CreateRenderPass2 and two subpasses.) Sounds like that\u0027s not helpful on desktop GPUs.\n\nOpen question then is what\u0027s best for TBRs that don\u0027t advertise MSRTSS but still need to load previously written multisample data? We could keep forcing them into the subpass path, but then we may need an internal hueristic to prevent advertising the LoadResolveTexture feature on IMRs. Or we could remove it entirely and hope that newer hardware embraces MSRTSS more? (I know from [this helpful fellow](https://medium.com/androiddevelopers/multisampled-anti-aliasing-for-almost-free-on-tile-based-rendering-hardware-21794c479cb9) that the ideal situation is to not load/store the MSAA target at all and use transient textures, but Graphite can\u0027t always do that.)",
      "parentUuid": "021166d6_ee667363",
      "revId": "abbcbf94a1d3e9ee8c789632f83b931a13d37c1e",
      "serverId": "dd02978d-1a8e-36d7-bcc0-a5723e5c0abd"
    },
